<?php

use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

/**
 * P01-SEC-13 -- PREPARED, NOT APPLIED. `customers.password_otp` cannot hold what the web
 * password-reset flow writes to it.
 *
 * ========================================================================================
 * THE DEFECT
 * ========================================================================================
 * database/migrations/2023_12_15_112855_create_customers_table.php:39 declares
 *
 *     $table->integer('password_otp')->nullable();
 *
 * and no migration anywhere in this repository alters it (verified: `password_otp` appears in
 * that one migration file and nowhere else under database/migrations).
 *
 * app/Http/Controllers/Customer/Auth/CustomerLoginController.php:154 writes
 *
 *     $customer->password_otp = Hash::make((string) $otp);
 *
 * -- a 60-character bcrypt string into an INTEGER column.
 *
 * WHAT EACH ENGINE DOES. SQLite's type affinity accepts the string unchanged, which is why the
 * test suite has never noticed. MySQL cannot: with `sql_mode` including STRICT_TRANS_TABLES the
 * INSERT/UPDATE raises error 1366 ("Incorrect integer value"); without it the value is coerced
 * to 0 and a warning is issued.
 *
 * NOT EXECUTED HERE, AND THAT MATTERS. This container has the PDO mysql client driver but no
 * MySQL server, so the engine behaviour above is established from the declared column type plus
 * documented MySQL semantics -- NOT by running it. Confirming it against the real server is the
 * first precondition below.
 *
 * ========================================================================================
 * IT FAILS CLOSED, SO THIS IS AVAILABILITY AND NOT A BYPASS
 * ========================================================================================
 * Read the verify step at CustomerLoginController.php:390-417 in order:
 *
 *   :391  `empty($customer->password_otp)` -- a coerced 0 is empty, so the request is refused
 *         with "No active verification code found."
 *   :413  `Hash::check($request->otp, $customer->password_otp)` is never reached, and would
 *         return false anyway: '0' is not a parsable bcrypt hash.
 *
 * So on MySQL the web reset is BROKEN, not exploitable: no OTP can ever verify and no customer
 * can complete a web password reset. In strict mode the send step 500s before that.
 *
 * ========================================================================================
 * WHY THE SMALLEST SAFE SOLUTION IS NOT THIS MIGRATION ALONE
 * ========================================================================================
 * The verify step has NO ATTEMPT LIMIT, and neither of the two POST routes
 * (routes/customer.php:118-131, `customer.password.otp.send` and `customer.password.otp.verify`)
 * carries a throttle -- the sibling login route at :102-104 does. The 5-minute expiry at :399
 * is the only bound on guessing, so an attacker may guess a 6-digit code as fast as the server
 * will answer, and re-send for a fresh code without limit.
 *
 * While the column is an INTEGER none of that is reachable, because nothing verifies at all.
 * APPLYING THIS MIGRATION BY ITSELF WOULD TURN A DEAD FLOW INTO A BRUTE-FORCEABLE ONE. The
 * attempt limit and the throttles are therefore part of the fix, not a follow-up; they are
 * implemented on this branch (CustomerLoginController::verifyPasswordOtp and
 * routes/customer.php) and are safe to carry while the flow is still inert.
 *
 * ========================================================================================
 * WHAT THIS MIGRATION DOES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
 * ========================================================================================
 * It widens the column to a nullable VARCHAR(255) -- enough for bcrypt's 60 characters with room
 * for argon2id (about 97) should the hashing driver ever change.
 *
 * IT DELETES AND REWRITES NOTHING. No customer row is touched; `up()` is one DDL statement.
 * MySQL converts existing INTEGER values to their decimal strings, and every such value is
 * already inert: a stale number in that column is refused at :399 by the 5-minute expiry on
 * `password_otp_datetime`, and would fail `Hash::check()` in any case because it is not a
 * parsable hash. Nulling them would be defensible housekeeping; it is not NECESSARY, and an
 * unnecessary UPDATE across the customer table is not what this change should be.
 *
 * ========================================================================================
 * PRECONDITIONS, before an owner runs this on any server
 * ========================================================================================
 * 1. Confirm the live type: `SHOW COLUMNS FROM customers LIKE 'password_otp';` -- a server
 *    provisioned outside these migrations may already be VARCHAR, in which case this is a no-op
 *    and should simply be dropped.
 * 2. Confirm the attempt limit and the two route throttles are deployed in the SAME release.
 *    Without them this migration makes the flow worse than leaving it broken.
 * 3. Take a backup that has been restored at least once. The DDL rewrites the table on MySQL;
 *    on a large `customers` table that is a locking operation -- size it first.
 * 4. Run it in a maintenance window. An in-flight web reset started before the change will fail
 *    and the customer must request a new code.
 *
 * ROLLBACK IS LOSSY AND SAYS SO. Narrowing VARCHAR back to INTEGER cannot represent a stored
 * bcrypt hash: MySQL would coerce it to 0 (or error in strict mode). `down()` therefore clears
 * non-numeric values first, which invalidates any reset in flight at that moment. That is the
 * honest cost of reversing, not a defect in the reversal.
 *
 * Execution evidence, against a real schema in an isolated connection:
 * tests/Feature/Security/CustomerPasswordOtpColumnMigrationTest.
 */
return new class extends Migration
{
    public function up(): void
    {
        if (! Schema::hasTable('customers') || ! Schema::hasColumn('customers', 'password_otp')) {
            return;
        }

        Schema::table('customers', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->string('password_otp', 255)->nullable()->change();
        });
    }

    public function down(): void
    {
        if (! Schema::hasTable('customers') || ! Schema::hasColumn('customers', 'password_otp')) {
            return;
        }

        // Anything that is not a plain integer cannot survive the narrowing. Cleared rather than
        // coerced to 0, so the reversal cannot leave a column full of zeros that the verify step
        // would read as "a code exists".
        \Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB::table('customers')
            ->whereNotNull('password_otp')
            ->get(['id', 'password_otp'])
            ->each(function ($row) {
                if (! preg_match('/^-?[0-9]+$/', (string) $row->password_otp)) {
                    \Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB::table('customers')
                        ->where('id', $row->id)
                        ->update(['password_otp' => null]);
                }
            });

        Schema::table('customers', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->integer('password_otp')->nullable()->change();
        });
    }
};
